Yang2025.pdf.md:17:Self-limited epilepsy with centrotemporal spikes is the most common pediatric epilepsy, characterized by an age-dependent onset that typically arises during childhood brain development and is followed by remission at puberty. However, the heterogeneity in children's brain development at the individual level complicates the challenge of personalized treatment. Our goal is to quantify individual deviations from the normative range of brain morphometric variation in children with Self-limited epilepsy with centrotemporal spikes and to assess their associations with clinical manifestations and cognitive functions. We have developed sex-specific normative models on regional subcortical volume, cortical thickness, and surface area data from 457 healthy children sourced from two datasets. These models were then utilized to map the brain morphometric deviations of children with Self-limited epilepsy with centrotemporal spikes ( n = 187) and sex- and age-matched healthy controls ( n = 108) from another dataset. In the Self-limited epilepsy with centrotemporal spikes group, children exhibited a higher proportion of regions with infra-normal deviations in subcortical volumes, the number of regions with normative deviations correlated with disease duration, seizure frequency, and Raven's total score. Our findings suggest that a few extreme distributions of heterogeneous brain morphometric deviations are present in a minority of individuals, emphasizing the need to monitor brain abnormalities throughout the course of the disease.
Yang2025.pdf.md:19:Keywords : brain development; brain morphology; individual-level heterogeneity; normative modeling; self-limited epilepsy with centrotemporal spikes.
Yang2025.pdf.md:25:Normative modeling offers powerful tools for assessing and predicting brain measures in individuals who deviate from the patterns observed in a normative reference population (Marquand et al. 2016; Gaiser et al. 2024). Conditioned by age, it furnishes estimates of neuroimaging phenotypes, including brain morphometry derived from structural and diffusion magnetic resonance image (MRI) data. For instance, normative modeling has been employed to describe brain volumetric development in infants (Dimitrova et al. 2021), and it has further established a correlation between deviations from normative development in preterm infants and factors such as respiratory, nutrition, birth weight, and neurocognition. Normative models have also been harnessed to create brain charts spanning the human lifespan, which have consolidated the largest neuroimaging datasets, mapped typical and atypical brain development and aging, and unveiled patterns of neuroanatomical variation across neurological and psychiatric disorders (Bethlehem et al. 2022). When applied to clinical phenotypes, studies have mapped the biological heterogeneity underlying the mental disorder and neurodevelopmental disorders, detecting individualized region deviations that differ from those found in the case-control study (Marquand et al. 2016; Rutherford et al. 2022a; Shan et al. 2022). Previous research has suggested that normative deviations outperform raw brain measures in estimating clinical conditions (Parkes et al. 2021). The accumulation of large sample datasets enables more precise normative distributions to be estimated (Bethlehem et al. 2022). For example, the CentileBrain framework provides an approach to normative modeling of brain morphometric measures through systematic empirical benchmarking and the identification of parameters that optimize model performance (Ge et al. 2024). Collectively, by providing statistical inferences relative to the normative range, parsing individual-level heterogeneity in clinical cohorts can expand the biological implications.
Yang2025.pdf.md:33:Previous studies on brain morphometry have reported atypical neuroanatomy in children with SeLECTS. Children with SeLECTS exhibited increasing thalamocortical structural connectivity within the motor circuit with age (Kwon et al. 2022) and showed increased regional gyrification index in the right posterior cingulate gyrus and increased sulcal depth in the right anterior cingulate gyrus and medial prefrontal cortex (Yin et al. 2024). Stratification of MRI data by age has provided evidence for variations in cortical thickness and white matter in children with SeLECTS (Smith et al. 2021). Furthermore, another study found larger gray matter volumes in the corticostriatal circuitry at the onset of epilepsy (Kim et al. 2015). The shape alterations of the putamen and caudate were also confirmed, and their relation with cognition depends on the side of epilepsy focus (Shakeri et al. 2017). While these case-control studies have detected group effects on brain morphometric measures,they do not quantify the interindividual variability that differs from case-control findings, nor do they identify potential regions that altered in a subset of children with SeLECTS. The use of normative modeling in this study compensates for limitations in these case-control studies and consider the following two points. First, the onset of children with SeLECTS often occurs during their critical period of brain development, and vulnerability in this process may be inferred from deviations from normative distributions. Second, neuroanatomical deviations may aid in monitoring possible language and cognitive dysfunction.
Yang2025.pdf.md:35:It is unknown whether morphometric measures deviations from healthy variation are present in individuals with SeLECTS and whether they are associated with clinical manifestation or cognitive function. In the current study, we employed normative modeling to estimate individual-level brain morphometric measures in children with SeLECTS. We hypothesized that children with SeLECTS would exhibit varying degrees of heterogeneity compared to the normal population. To test these hypotheses, we utilized the CentileBrain framework to derive normative model parameters based on the developing Chinese Color Nest Project (depCCNP) dataset and subsequently estimated deviations in brain morphometric measures to characterize the differences between children with SeLECTS and the normative range. Finally, we examined whether regional deviations in brain morphometric measures from the normative range were associated with clinical manifestation or cognitive function.
Yang2025.pdf.md:45:To ensure the generalizability of the normative models, we included additional normal data to train the models. The data for model training consists of two samples: the CKG sample and the PEK sample. Samples from the two different centers are both sourced from the developing Chinese Color Nest Project (devCCNP). The devCCNP resource is accessible within the Chinese Color Nest Project (CCNP)-Lifespan Brain-Mind Development Data Community (https://ccnp.scidb.cn) hosted by the Science Data Bank. The two samples are combined to train the normative models, and the potential impact of site on performance is addressed by treating site as a random factor and using ComBatGAM (Pomponio et al. 2020) for site harmonization. These normal data comprise two samples: the CKG sample and the PEK sample. The two samples were combined to train the normative models, and both originate. This project enrolled typically developing school-age Chinese children and adolescents, and this study comprised 241 boys and 216 girls, aged 3 to 18 years, with a mean age of 10.44 ± 3.11 years. All normal participants had no history of neurological disorder or psychiatric illness and no gross abnormalities on brain MRI images. This project was approved by the Institutional Review Board of the Institute of Psychology, Chinese Academy of Sciences. Written informed consent was obtained from one of the participants' legal guardians, and written assent was obtained from the participants.
Yang2025.pdf.md:49:Structural images were acquired on a Siemens Trio Tim 3.0tesla MRI scanner at Jinling Hospital. We used foam padding to minimize head motion. All participants were required to keep their eyes closed and to keep their heads still. Highresolution T1-weighted MRI data were acquired in a sagittal orientation using a magnetization-prepared rapid gradientecho sequence (repetition time/echo time=2,300/2.98 ms, flip angle=9 ◦ , field of view=256 × 256 mm 2 , matrix size = 256 × 256, slice thickness=1 mm, no interslice gap, and 176 slices). We also used T1-weighted MRI data provided by the devCCNP. Full details for the devCCNP have been published (Fan et al. 2023).
Yang2025.pdf.md:53:The following processing was applied to both the SeLECTS casecontrol cohort and the devCCNP normal datasets. Structural MRI processing pipelines were primarily built using FreeSurfer (Fischl et al. 2001) and FSL (Smith et al. 2004). T1-weight data processing included cortical segmentation and surface reconstruction analyses. Anatomical surfaces were generated from the individual T1w image in native space using the recon-all command in FreeSurfer. Estimation of individual morphometric measures included the total intracranial volume, regional subcortical volume ( n =14), regional cortical thickness ( n =68), and surface area ( n =68).
Yang2025.pdf.md:57:To ensure generalizability and robustness, we followed the normative modeling framework provided by CentileBrain (Ge et al. 2024). Using age and sex as covariates, normative models for each regional morphometric measure were developed utilizing
Yang2025.pdf.md:72:Note: Values are mean ± standard deviation (SD). Abbreviations: SeLECTS, Self-limited epilepsy with Centrotemporal Spikes; HC, healthy controls; ND, no data. The χ 2 value for gender distribution was obtained by the chi-square test. The U values were obtained by Mann-Whitney tests. a Missed clinical information.
Yang2025.pdf.md:74:data from a multisite sample of 457 healthy individuals (47.3% female; aged 3 to 18 years) from the devCCNP. These data were used to construct the models, primarily based on the following considerations. First, while the original CentileBrain model encompasses a lifespan from 3 to 90 years, our study specifically focuses on the distribution of brain morphology during children's development (3 to 18 years). Secondly, although the original CentileBrain collected samples from diverse regions and ethnicities globally, our subsequent analysis of childhood epilepsy predominantly centered on the Chinese population. Leveraging the CentileBrain pipeline, we opted for multivariable fractional polynomial regression implemented with the mfp package in R. This algorithm can determine the functional form of the predictor by testing a wide family of shapes and multiple turning points while providing a good fit at the extremes of the covariates. The optimal covariate combination included site harmonization (ComBat-GAM) ( Pomponio et al. 2020) and the incorporation of intracranial volume, mean cortical thickness, and mean surface area in the regional models for subcortical volume, cortical thickness, and surface area regional measures, respectively.
Yang2025.pdf.md:78:The sex-specific and region-specific model parameters by the CentileBrain normative modeling framework were subsequently applied to each region morphometric measure of the children with SeLECTS and healthy controls. The normative deviation for each regional morphometric measure was defined as the z -scores:
Yang2025.pdf.md:86:Subsequently, we computed the averaged normative deviation for each regional morphometric measure and derived averaged deviation scores specifically for subcortical volume, cortical thickness, and surface area. Additionally, we determined the percentage of participants who exhibited an averaged infra-normal deviation or supra-normal deviation, which reflected a general trend of deviations falling below or exceeding the reference range, respectively.
Yang2025.pdf.md:112:and healthy controls were highly overlapping ( Fig. 1; Figs. S1 and S2 in Supplementary Materials). The cortical thickness deviations in the SeLECTS group exhibited extreme distributions in certain regions, such as the bilateral frontal lobe and inferior temporal, lateral, and medial orbitofrontal gyri.
Yang2025.pdf.md:114:To further compare the differences between the groups, we calculated the percentage of children with SeLECTS and healthy controls who had infra- and supra-normal z -scores for each regional morphometric measure (Fig. 2). For subcortical volume, the regions with infra-normal z -scores were ranged from 1.6% to 8.6% in children with SeLECTS and from 0.9% to 4.6% in healthy controls; the corresponding ranges for supra-normal z -scores were 2.7% to 7.5% in children with SeLECTS and 1.9% to 6.5% in healthy controls (Fig. 2A and B). A higher percentage of infra-normal z -scores was observed in the left accumbens among children with SeLECTS and in the right thalamus among healthy controls. Similarly, a higher percentage of supra-normal z -scores was observed in the right hippocampus among children with SeLECTS and in the left accumbens among healthy controls (Fig. 2C).
Yang2025.pdf.md:118:Regarding surface area, the regions with infra-normal z -scores ranged from 0.5% to 13.4% in the SeLECTS group and from 0.9% to 13.0% in the control group; the corresponding ranges for supranormal z -scores were 1.1% to 9.6% in the SeLECTS group and 0.9% to 10.2% in the control group (Fig. 2A and B). A higher percentage of infra-normal z -scores was observed in the right rostral middle frontal gyrus among children with SeLECTS and the right posterior cingulate in healthy controls; a higher percentage of supra-normal was observed in the left entorhinal among children with SeLECTS and the right frontal pole among healthy controls (Fig. 2C). There were no significant group differences in any brain region in terms of the percentage of participants with regional infra- or supra-normal z -scores (Tables S1-S3 in Supplement Materials).
