114:To further compare the differences between the groups, we calculated the percentage of children with SeLECTS and healthy controls who had infra- and supra-normal z -scores for each regional morphometric measure (Fig. 2). For subcortical volume, the regions with infra-normal z -scores were ranged from 1.6% to 8.6% in children with SeLECTS and from 0.9% to 4.6% in healthy controls; the corresponding ranges for supra-normal z -scores were 2.7% to 7.5% in children with SeLECTS and 1.9% to 6.5% in healthy controls (Fig. 2A and B). A higher percentage of infra-normal z -scores was observed in the left accumbens among children with SeLECTS and in the right thalamus among healthy controls. Similarly, a higher percentage of supra-normal z -scores was observed in the right hippocampus among children with SeLECTS and in the left accumbens among healthy controls (Fig. 2C).
116:For cortical thickness, the regions with infra-normal z -scores ranged from 1.1% to 11.8% in the SeLECTS group and from 0.9% to 9.3% in the control group; the corresponding ranges for supranormal z -scores were 0.5% to 13.9% in the SeLECTS group and 0.9% to 13.9% in the control group (Fig. 2A and B). A higher percentage of infra-normal z -scores was observed in the right postcentral gyrus in both groups, and a higher percentage of supra-normal was shown in the right pars triangularis in both groups (Fig. 2C).
118:Regarding surface area, the regions with infra-normal z -scores ranged from 0.5% to 13.4% in the SeLECTS group and from 0.9% to 13.0% in the control group; the corresponding ranges for supranormal z -scores were 1.1% to 9.6% in the SeLECTS group and 0.9% to 10.2% in the control group (Fig. 2A and B). A higher percentage of infra-normal z -scores was observed in the right rostral middle frontal gyrus among children with SeLECTS and the right posterior cingulate in healthy controls; a higher percentage of supra-normal was observed in the left entorhinal among children with SeLECTS and the right frontal pole among healthy controls (Fig. 2C). There were no significant group differences in any brain region in terms of the percentage of participants with regional infra- or supra-normal z -scores (Tables S1-S3 in Supplement Materials).
