    88	## Estimation of normative deviations and observed morphometry
    89	
    90	To evaluate whether normative deviations outperform observed morphometric measures, we employed support vector classification (SVC) models to predict the diagnostic status of children with SeLECTS. All the data (deviations of the three types of observed measures) were used together as inputs to classify using the SVC model. The regional z -scores and observed morphometric data were independently input into separate SVC models, utilizing 5-fold cross-validation implemented with a linear kernel from the scikit-learn package (version 1.2.2) (Rutherford et al. 2022b) following this procedure (Rutherford et al. 2022b). The area under the receiver operating characteristic curve was averaged across all folds. Additionally, we separately used each of the three measurements as inputs to compare the model performance.
    91	
    92	## Statistical analysis
    93	
    94	Group comparisons of the percentage of participants were conducted using the 2-proportion z -test implemented in R studio, while group comparisons of normative z -scores and observed measures employed the two-sample t -test. The association between the number of regions with infraor supra-normal deviations and clinical manifestations was examined using the Spearman correlation; the association between the averaged deviations of each morphometric measure and clinical manifestations was examined using Pearson's correlation. All statistical tests were considered significant at a threshold of P &lt; 0.05, and false discovery rate (FDR) correction was applied to account for multiple comparisons.
